WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.977
The first published NIST PQC standards are not the end of the migration story.

2
00:00:04.977 --> 00:00:12.941
Additional algorithms remain important because cryptographic migration is a long program, not a single product upgrade.

3
00:00:12.941 --> 00:00:21.594
Falcon was selected as an additional digital signature algorithm path, and NIST has continued work toward a separate standard for it.

4
00:00:21.594 --> 00:00:28.333
The standardized naming and final implementation expectations should be tracked as guidance matures.

5
00:00:28.333 --> 00:00:32.161
HQC was selected as a backup key-establishment algorithm.

6
00:00:32.161 --> 00:00:40.738
Backup choices matter because organizations should not rely on a single design family forever when the ecosystem is still maturing.

7
00:00:40.738 --> 00:00:53.373
A backup KEM can provide another option if future analysis, implementation experience, or operational requirements create a need for diversity beyond the primary key-establishment standard.

8
00:00:53.373 --> 00:00:58.427
Primary choices, backup choices, and future standards play different roles.

9
00:00:58.427 --> 00:01:04.706
A primary standard is the main reference point for near-term implementation and vendor support.

10
00:01:04.706 --> 00:01:08.688
A backup choice helps manage longer-term risk and diversity.

11
00:01:08.688 --> 00:01:15.426
A future standard may become relevant as products, validation programs, and protocol support develop.

12
00:01:15.426 --> 00:01:19.025
Treating every status as identical creates confusion.

13
00:01:19.025 --> 00:01:26.759
Migration programs should track these algorithms without chasing every draft as if it were already production-ready.

14
00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:40.696
The right posture is informed readiness: know what is coming, ask vendors about roadmaps, design systems that can change algorithms, and avoid hard-coding assumptions that will make the next transition expensive.

15
00:01:40.696 --> 00:01:46.975
PQC readiness is partly about adopting standards and partly about building the ability to adapt.
